\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The exact Hurwitz partition and loop averages}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Fix mass #{m=5} and weight #{w=1} in the
[one-vertex Hurwitz model](fdip-00ZA), and take the
[first-loop observables](fdip-00ZO). The determinant
identity of \ref{fdip-00ZD}, the counts of
\ref{fdip-00ZK} and the trace totals of
\ref{fdip-00ZL} give
##{\begin{aligned}
Z(5)&=\sum_{s,t}c_{s,t}\bigl((25-s)^2-t\bigr)^4\\
&=24323693375424,\\[2pt]
\Phi_5(\trace_F L_U)&=\sum_{s,t}c_{s,t}(4-s)
                   \bigl((25-s)^2-t\bigr)^4\\
&=17822802235392.
\end{aligned}}
The conditional dressed coefficient of \ref{fdip-00ZQ}
multiplies the determinant by its scalar with one
factor canceled. Thus
##{\begin{aligned}
\Phi_5(P)&=-5\sum_{s,t}c_{s,t}(4-s)(25-s)
                   \bigl((25-s)^2-t\bigr)^3\\
&=-3639062949120.
\end{aligned}}
Each sum uses precisely the #{15} displayed rows.
Dividing numerator and denominator by #{192} gives
##{\langle\trace_F L_U\rangle_5
=\frac{92827094976}{126685902997},\qquad
\langle P\rangle_5
=-\frac{18953452860}{126685902997}.}
The positive denominator is justified in
\ref{fdip-00ZQ}.}

\p{These values use unnormalized counting on the
#{576} ordered pairs. Giving each pair uniform weight
#{1/576} divides #{Z(5)}, #{\Phi_5(\trace_F L_U)} and
#{\Phi_5(P)} by #{576}. The two normalized ratios
are unchanged.}

\p{For the same model, \ref{fdip-00VR} gives
#{\mathcal V\cong F^4} as a free right #{F}-module,
with rational rank #{16}. By \ref{fdip-00VN}, its
[universal right-action commutant](fdip-00VK) is
#{F^{\mathrm{op}}}, acting by internal right
multiplication; the geometric right-Clifford part is
#{\mathbb Q\bbI}. The internal commutant on a single
coefficient fiber is also #{F^{\mathrm{op}}}, by
\ref{fdip-00V5}.}
